---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
---

<Base
  title="Responsible use"
  description="How Feature Teardowns are made: open-source apps, public behaviour and lawful analysis. We never publish other companies' code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="label label-accent">Responsible use</p>
      <h1>We explain ideas. We don't hand out other people's code.</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        Feature Teardowns exists to teach how good software works. Here is where our explanations come from, and the
        lines we don't cross.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where our explanations come from</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Open-source apps.</strong> Where a feature exists in an app whose code is published openly, we learn
          from that.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Public behaviour.</strong> What anyone can see by using an app: what it shows, how it responds and
          what it does over time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lawful analysis.</strong> We study software only in ways the law allows, using REA (Reverse Engineer
          Anything) to understand how a feature is put together.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What we never do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We never publish other companies' code.</li>
        <li>We never share private keys, passwords, internal documents or anything else that isn't ours to share.</li>
        <li>We don't explain how to break into accounts, get around payment, or get past an app's security.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What a teardown is</h2>
      <p>
        A teardown describes the idea behind a feature in plain English, with a diagram we drew ourselves. The
        "Build your own" prompt asks an AI coding agent to write a fresh version from that description. Nothing in it
        is copied from the original app.
      </p>

      <h2>What we ask of you</h2>
      <p>
        Use these teardowns to learn and to build your own things. Don't use them to clone someone's product, copy
        their branding, or pass your version off as theirs.
      </p>

      {site.xUrl && (
        <>
          <h2>Spotted a problem?</h2>
          <p>
            If you think something on this site crosses one of these lines, tell us and we'll look at it.
            <a href={site.xUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Message us on X</a>.
          </p>
        </>
      )}
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
